"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"C057\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer correctly gives the 75-day Rule 485(a) period for a new series of an existing trust. It also says effectiveness 'only legally registers the series' and is not a launch guarantee. However, it wrongly says a new trust faces the same 75-day period, so it fails the 'not initial trust automatic clock' part.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Optional point. The answer does not say shares cannot be sold before effectiveness and gives no filed example. Optional omissions do not affect the grade.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"The answer lists operational steps that add time beyond effectiveness: exchange listing approval, seed capital and AP agreements, service-provider setup, NSCC/DTC readiness, and resolving staff comments. The optional 95-day limit is not mentioned.\"}],\"central_error\":\"Offending quote: 'A brand-new trust files Form N-1A under Section 8(a) and Rule 485(a) and typically also faces the 75-day period.' This matches the central error '75-day automatic Rule 485 clock applies to initial trust registration.'\",\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer handles the new-series 75-day period well and explains that effectiveness is not a launch guarantee. But it explicitly applies the Rule 485(a) 75-day period to a brand-new trust's initial registration. Initial registration is not a Rule 485 post-effective amendment. Under the scoring rule, any explicit central error requires a grade of wrong.\"}","ttft_ms":3500,"type":"result","duration_ms":7588,"uuid":"a0835e27-f56f-4b2b-adbd-589eb74b4175","ttft_stream_ms":1520,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":1520,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
